This is a program that fits the Murnaghan,
F. D. Murnaghan, Proc. Natl. Acad. Sci. 30, 244 (1944),
or Birch-Murnaghan, F. Birch J. Geophys. Res. 83, 1257 (1978),
equations of state to a series E_j(V_i) of calculated
total energies for structures j and volumes i.

It identifies which structure is stable at a given pressure, gives the
transition pressures, and generates plots of E_j(V) and V(p).
For each structure it also gives the equilibrium volume V_0, the energy
at that volume E_0, the bulk modulus at equilibrium B(V_0)
and its derivative dB/dp at V_0.

The description of the data file (eqst.dat), of how to compile and run
the program, and of the output is in the user guide,

   Doc/UserGuide/user_guide.tex  (or user_guide.pdf)

section Toolbox, subsection "Equation of state".

In short, the subroutines are in the libpw library, so compile with the
same compiler used for the library, for example

$ gfortran -o eqst.exe eqst.f90 ../../libpw.a

and run in the directory with the data file eqst.dat

$ eqst.exe

If eqst.dat does not exist the program asks for the name of the data file.

To see the plots install gnuplot and run

$ gnuplot eofv_com.gp
$ gnuplot eqst_com.gp

or, with python and matplotlib,

$ python3 eofv_plot.py
$ python3 eqst_plot.py

The original code is from David Wood in the early 80s.  It has been
modified many times since then.
